Yield a host turn where the loop would block in epoll_pwait.

On the host JS-engine arm the guest's JS runs on the engine that hosts the
kernel, and that engine delivers finalizers, clears kept objects and runs its
idle work only when the worker task the guest occupies ends. uv__io_poll is
where the loop goes idle, so it is where that task should end: one call to
visorjs_yield_turn (the shim's entry, declared in <visorjs-yield.h>) unwinds
the guest stack to the worker, the worker takes one host task, and the
fork-rewind replay brings the loop back to this exact call. Idle guest time
becomes host time; a busy loop pays nothing, because a poll with timeout 0 is
skipped.

The turn REPLACES the wait rather than preceding it. The call carries the
epoll fd and the computed timeout: the kernel arms an async readiness wait on
that fd, and the worker parks on it -- for at most the timeout -- with no
guest stack under it, so the idle time is the host engine's. When the call
returns 1 the wait has already happened, so the epoll_pwait below runs with a
timeout of 0 and collects whatever became ready. A zero timeout also takes the
loop's own `update_timeout` path, which is exactly the upstream path for a
poll that has spent its budget: events break out, no events returns, and
uv_run re-enters with a freshly computed timeout.

The backend refuses the turn -- returning 0 and costing nothing -- on the
mozjs arm, before V8::Initialize has negotiated the host arm (node's
delayed-task thread polls before it), when guest JS is on the stack, and when
the kernel will not arm the readiness wait; the loop then blocks in
epoll_pwait exactly as it did before.

--- a/deps/uv/src/unix/linux.c
+++ b/deps/uv/src/unix/linux.c
@@ -53,6 +53,10 @@
 #include <sys/sysmacros.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <sys/utsname.h>
+
+#if defined(__wasm__)
+# include <visorjs-yield.h>
+#endif
 #include <time.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 
@@ -1448,6 +1452,23 @@
         uv__epoll_ctl_flush(epollfd, ctl, &prep);
 
     uv__io_poll_prepare(loop, NULL, timeout);
+#if defined(__wasm__)
+    /* The host turn, in place of the block below. On the host JS-engine arm
+     * the guest's JS runs on the engine hosting the kernel, and that engine
+     * delivers finalizers and runs its idle work only when the worker task
+     * the guest occupies ends. This is where the loop goes idle, so this is
+     * where that task ends: the kernel arms an async readiness wait on
+     * epollfd, the worker parks on it for at most `timeout`, and the replay
+     * brings the loop back to this call. The wait has then already happened,
+     * so the epoll_pwait below collects with a timeout of 0.
+     *
+     * Refused (0) on the mozjs arm, before V8::Initialize, with guest JS on
+     * the stack, or when the kernel will not arm the wait: the loop blocks
+     * below as it always did. A timeout of 0 never waits, so it is skipped. */
+    if (timeout != 0 &&
+        visorjs_yield_turn(VISORJS_YIELD_POLL, timeout, epollfd) == 1)
+      timeout = 0;
+#endif
     nfds = epoll_pwait(epollfd, events, ARRAY_SIZE(events), timeout, sigmask);
     uv__io_poll_check(loop, NULL);
 
